  "behavior_evidence": [
    "Twenty native cases record twenty native states and two separately labeled recovered states. Eighteen native cases compare directly: empty/equal no-ops, retained growth, move/copy relocation, minimum/doubled capacity, default/fill tails, ordered shrinking/zeroing and first-constructor failures.",
    "Late default/fill construction failures leave two extra live payloads outside native Size. Native rows explicitly retain those resources. A separate fixture repair destroys each unpublished tail object before the recovered row; Rust automatic rollback matches only this recovered row. Tests additionally assert the native resource count exceeds Rust by the completed tail count.",
    "Row comparisons cover length, capacity, allocation identity, constructor/destructor order, moved-from source state, original/spare-slot destructor addresses, donor state and live payload counts. Growth relocation source destruction happens only after every replacement element is constructed.",
    "Safe resize_with_relocation composes reserve_with_relocation and existing resize_with_constructor/LengthRollback. It adds no unsafe code, allocator/lifetime machinery, layout/Drop change or Clone/Default bound.",
    "Extra Rust tests prove borrowed owner lifetimes, callback skipping and capture release on equal size, safe remaining-tail cleanup after a shrinking destructor panic, and safe replacement cleanup/unchanged registered storage/skipped tail construction after relocation panic. The last two are explicit safe recovery extensions.",
    "Original ResizeNonTrivial and Resize_WithValue_NonTrivial retain their inputs and assertions while calling explicit move/default/copy policies. All forty-nine original declarations pass.",
    "Baseline previous Append candidate library rejects the new method; candidate compiles/runs it. Covariance, Send/Sync/Unpin and unwind traits remain unchanged."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Native late-constructor-failure states retain live unpublished tail values. Rust rolls them back. The two recovered fixture rows include explicit extra native cleanup and do not establish direct native failure parity.",
    "Native shrink destructor exceptions can leave dead registered slots and undestroyed later tail objects; Rust truncate safely lowers length and drops remaining tail. Native invalid lifetime state is not exposed by this safe adapter.",
    "Native growth exceptions may leak unpublished replacement storage/objects; existing Rust relocation cleanup is retained. Growth destructor failures are not claimed equivalent.",
    "Callbacks select native type operations explicitly. Returning values does not model address-dependent in-place construction. Callback-time aliased metadata observations, allocator hooks, ABI layout and zero-sized native equivalence remain outside scope.",
    "No general protected grow/constructAt/destroyRange parity or global API/test ledger credit is added. Class/kernel full parity is not established."
  ],
